  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 349 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThe current cold front situated just NW of MO will continue to sag \nsouthward before becoming quasi-stationary over northern/central MO \nby the afternoon tomorrow. Despite earlier convection along the cold \nfront, an increasingly inhibited environment with an unfavorable \nsynoptic environment has decreased PoPs through the overnight. High \ntemperatures behind the front fall to the high 60s/low 70s, \nwhile areas south of the front remain around the 80s. Continued \nspread in HREF members on temperature solutions during the \nafternoon (~4-6 degF spreads) remains across the northern half \nof MO. However, this corridor of spread narrows through the \ncourse of the day, increasing confidence in the position of the \nfront eventually settling over central MO. South of the boundary\ncloser to the US-50 corridor, low- level southerly flow will \ncreate a narrow corridor of elevated instability by the late \nafternoon. Capping will prevent any initiation earlier on in the\nday, but guidance increasingly indicates the erosion of the cap\nby the late afternoon. However, our other ingredients for \nthunderstorm development (vertical wind shear and lift) remain \nmarginal at best, with better synoptic forcing (a shortwave, a \nLLJ, etc.) remaining displaced from the boundary's location. \nThis creates a setup where, although convection is not out of \nthe question with surface convergence along the boundary by the \nmid-late afternoon, the risk of severe activity remains low. By \nthis evening, low-level WAA out ahead of a shortwave will \nreinvigorate chances for precipitation (40-60%) as the boundary \nlifts back northward. Convective guidance resolves this activity\nas widespread scattered showers, with building convective \ninhibition as the nocturnal boundary layer establishes itself \ndespite increasingly favorable shear profiles and elevated \ninstability. This activity looks to persist through early Friday\nmorning/afternoon as the boundary stalls out once again over \nnorthern MO, with overall PoPs around 15-30% through the \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA stout mid-level ridge persists over the eastern third of the \nCONUS, keeping the forecast area within the periphery of its flow. \nAnother shortwave following the atmospheric flow northward from the \nsouthern Plains will increase PoPs (70-90%) Friday evening, \nbolstered by dCVA at the base of an oncoming mid-level trough. This \nlow amplitude trough will act to push the mid-level ridge axis \nfurther off towards the east. However, persistent low-level WAA and \ndCVA south of I-70 will keep PoPs around 30-50% through Saturday \nafternoon/evening, which isn't great news for any folks looking to \ngo out for Halloween. In (slightly?) better news, surface winds out \nof the E/SE coupled with increased cloud cover will help \ntemperatures to fall to the high 60s/low 70s areawide by Saturday, \nwhich (although still seasonally high) is much better than continued \n80 degree temperatures into November. By Sunday, PoPs look to clear \nout, with semi-zonal flow as the ridge axis bends off towards the \nnorthwest and a mid-level trough over the western CONUS begins its \ntrek eastward. Models continue to indicate the development of a \ndeepening surface cyclone over the central CONUS, which will \ncontinue to leave the beginning of next week looking wet as the \ncyclone lifts off towards the northeast. Synoptic model guidance \nbegins to deviate by the later part of the forecast period on the \nlocation of the trough axis over the central CONUS, and whether the \ntrough closes off or continues to propagate along the \natmospheric flow. This leads to greater deviations in PoPs by \nnext Wednesday, which decreases overall confidence in solutions \nfor precipitation in the extended forecast. Greater advection of\ndry, cooler air from the northwest behind the initial surface \ncyclone will continue to lower temperatures into the early days \nof November to the high 50s/low 60s areawide.",
